  "query_preview": "(This evening through Wednesday)...\nIssued at 159 PM EDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nSurface analysis this afternoon shows strong high pressure centered \nover the northern plains states with a surface ridge nosing SE to \nIndiana and Ohio. This was resulting in north to NE surface flow \nacross Central Indiana and cooler temperatures compared to recent \nweather. Dew points were in the upper 40s and low 50s. GOES19 shows \njust some scattered and broken CU across the area. Aloft, ridging \nwas found over the western CONUS, with NW flow spilling from western \nCanada into the Ohio Valley.\n\nTonight through Friday...\n\nRelatively little change is expected during the period within the \nupper levels. Strong ridging is expected to continue to reside over \nthe western CONUS, resulting lee side subsidence. This will result \nin the strong high pressure system that is over the northern plains \nto slowly work its way SE through Friday. Cooler easterly surface \nflow is expected to persist on Thursday, before further veering \noccurs on Friday with flow from the southeast. This will allow highs \nthe next few days to return to near 80 degrees as the air mass \nmodifies. Humidity will also remain somewhat pleasant as dew points \nremain in the 50s due to the east and southeast component of the \nwinds.\n\nForecast soundings through the period continue to suggest a dry \ncolumn with potential for afternoon CU as convective temperatures \nare reached. This will result in partly cloudy, pleasant days and \nmostly clear nights.\n\nSaturday through Wednesday...\n\nDry weather with a warming trend is expected on Saturday and Sunday. \nThat warming trend will continue into Monday through Wednesday, but \nsmall chances for thunderstorms will be present.\n\nThe ongoing, persistent pattern of strong ridging aloft over the \nwestern CONUS will continue trough Sunday. This will will keep the \nNW flow aloft in place over Indiana along with ongoing subsidence. \nThe strong surface high will control our weather, but drift east and \nallow warmer, southerly flow as temperatures reach the 80s on \nSaturday and Near 90 on Sunday.\n\nModels continue to show strong ridging in place west of Indiana on \nMonday through Wednesday, blocking much in the way of upper support. \nHowever within the lower levels, low pressure moving through the \nGreat Lakes toward the middle Atlantic states suggest a cold front \nwill drag across Indiana late Monday into Tuesday. Minimal change in \nair mass appears in play as there is little change in temperature in \nthe wake of the front. Forecast soundings on Monday through \nWednesday show plenty of CAPE available, but very warm mid levels \nnear 13-14C at 700mb. Thus capping could be in play. For now, small \npops for diurnal convection and with the frontal passage will be \nincluded on Monday afternoon through Wednesday afternoon, but \nconfidence is low at this point. Highs will remain above normal \nearly next week, in the low to middle 90s.",
